#: src/features/RolePlay/scenarios/talking-to-a-doctor.ts:16
#. js-lingui-explicit-id
msgid ""
"Talking to a doctor can be difficult, especially when you need to describe "
"symptoms in another language. You may need to explain what you feel, when "
"the problem started, how serious it is, and how it affects your daily life.\n"
"\n"
"You do not need advanced medical vocabulary. Clear descriptions, specific "
"examples, and honest answers are usually more useful than trying to guess "
"the name of a condition.\n"
"\n"
"This roleplay is for language practice only. It cannot diagnose a medical "
"condition, recommend treatment, or replace professional medical care. If "
"you believe you may be experiencing a medical emergency, contact your local "
"emergency service.\n"
"\n"
"## Start the Consultation\n"
"\n"
"A doctor may begin by asking:\n"
"\n"
"- “What brings you in today?”\n"
"- “How can I help you?”\n"
"- “What seems to be the problem?”\n"
"- “Could you describe your symptoms?”\n"
"- “When did this begin?”\n"
"- “Have you experienced this before?”\n"
"\n"
"You can begin with a short summary:\n"
"\n"
"- “I’ve had a persistent cough for about a week.”\n"
"- “I’ve been experiencing pain in my lower back.”\n"
"- “I’ve been feeling unusually tired recently.”\n"
"- “I have a rash on my arm that is not improving.”\n"
"- “I’ve been getting headaches more often than usual.”\n"
"- “I’m here to discuss the results of my recent tests.”\n"
"- “I’m following up about a problem we discussed previously.”\n"
"\n"
"A useful opening structure is:\n"
"\n"
"> “I’ve been experiencing [symptom] for [length of time], and it is "
"[improving, getting worse, or staying the same].”\n"
"\n"
"For example:\n"
"\n"
"> “I’ve been experiencing pain in my right shoulder for about ten days, and "
"it seems to be getting worse.”\n"
"\n"
"## Describe Where the Problem Is\n"
"\n"
"Be as specific as possible about the location of pain or discomfort.\n"
"\n"
"Useful phrases include:\n"
"\n"
"- “The pain is in my lower back.”\n"
"- “It hurts on the right side of my chest.”\n"
"- “I feel discomfort behind my left knee.”\n"
"- “The pain starts in my neck and moves into my shoulder.”\n"
"- “The rash is mainly on my arms.”\n"
"- “My whole body feels sore.”\n"
"- “It is difficult to identify the exact location.”\n"
"- “The pain seems to move.”\n"
"\n"
"The doctor may ask:\n"
"\n"
"- “Can you point to where it hurts?”\n"
"- “Is it on one side or both sides?”\n"
"- “Does the pain move anywhere else?”\n"
"- “Is the discomfort near the surface or deeper inside?”\n"
"- “Is the affected area sensitive to touch?”\n"
"\n"
"## Explain When It Started\n"
"\n"
"The doctor will often ask when the symptoms began and whether anything "
"happened beforehand.\n"
"\n"
"You can say:\n"
"\n"
"- “It started this morning.”\n"
"- “I first noticed it three days ago.”\n"
"- “The symptoms began gradually.”\n"
"- “It started suddenly while I was exercising.”\n"
"- “It began after I returned from a trip.”\n"
"- “I noticed it after starting a new medication.”\n"
"- “I woke up with the pain.”\n"
"- “I cannot remember exactly when it started.”\n"
"\n"
"Useful questions you may hear:\n"
"\n"
"- “Did it begin suddenly or gradually?”\n"
"- “What were you doing when it started?”\n"
"- “Did anything unusual happen beforehand?”\n"
"- “Have the symptoms changed since then?”\n"
"- “Was there an injury?”\n"
"\n"
"## Describe How It Feels\n"
"\n"
"Try to describe the sensation rather than only saying that something hurts.\n"
"\n"
"Pain or discomfort may feel:\n"
"\n"
"- Sharp\n"
"- Dull\n"
"- Burning\n"
"- Throbbing\n"
"- Stabbing\n"
"- Aching\n"
"- Cramping\n"
"- Tight\n"
"- Heavy\n"
"- Tingling\n"
"- Numb\n"
"- Itchy\n"
"- Sensitive\n"
"- Tender\n"
"\n"
"Examples:\n"
"\n"
"- “It feels like a dull ache.”\n"
"- “I sometimes feel a sharp pain.”\n"
"- “There is a burning sensation.”\n"
"- “My fingers feel numb.”\n"
"- “The area is itchy and sensitive.”\n"
"- “It feels like pressure rather than pain.”\n"
"- “My chest feels tight.”\n"
"- “I have a tingling sensation in my hand.”\n"
"\n"
"If you do not know the exact word, describe it in another way:\n"
"\n"
"> “It feels as if something is pressing on the area.”\n"
"\n"
"## Explain How Severe It Is\n"
"\n"
"A doctor may ask you to rate pain or discomfort on a scale from zero to "
"ten.\n"
"\n"
"- **Zero** means no pain.\n"
"- **Ten** means the worst pain you can imagine.\n"
"\n"
"You can say:\n"
"\n"
"- “The pain is about a four out of ten.”\n"
"- “It is mild most of the time.”\n"
"- “It becomes severe in the evening.”\n"
"- “It is uncomfortable, but I can still work.”\n"
"- “The pain is strong enough to wake me up.”\n"
"- “It is difficult to walk because of it.”\n"
"- “The symptoms interfere with my daily activities.”\n"
"\n"
"It is also helpful to explain what you can or cannot do:\n"
"\n"
"- “I can walk, but climbing stairs is painful.”\n"
"- “I cannot concentrate at work.”\n"
"- “I have difficulty sleeping.”\n"
"- “I can eat normally.”\n"
"- “I become tired after minor physical activity.”\n"
"- “I have had to stop exercising.”\n"
"\n"
"## Explain How Often It Happens\n"
"\n"
"Symptoms may be constant or may come and go.\n"
"\n"
"Useful phrases include:\n"
"\n"
"- “The pain is constant.”\n"
"- “It comes and goes.”\n"
"- “It happens several times a day.”\n"
"- “It usually lasts for about twenty minutes.”\n"
"- “The symptoms are worse in the morning.”\n"
"- “It happens mainly after eating.”\n"
"- “It only happens when I exercise.”\n"
"- “I feel better after resting.”\n"
"- “There does not seem to be a pattern.”\n"
"\n"
"The doctor may ask:\n"
"\n"
"- “How long does each episode last?”\n"
"- “Does it happen every day?”\n"
"- “Is it worse at a particular time?”\n"
"- “Does anything trigger it?”\n"
"- “Does anything make it better?”\n"
"\n"
"## Describe What Makes It Better or Worse\n"
"\n"
"Explain whether movement, food, rest, medication, or another factor changes "
"your symptoms.\n"
"\n"
"You can say:\n"
"\n"
"- “It gets worse when I move.”\n"
"- “The pain increases when I take a deep breath.”\n"
"- “It feels better after I rest.”\n"
"- “Heat seems to help.”\n"
"- “The symptoms are worse after meals.”\n"
"- “It improves when I lie down.”\n"
"- “Pain relief helped temporarily.”\n"
"- “Nothing seems to make a difference.”\n"
"- “The problem becomes worse when I am stressed.”\n"
"\n"
"Useful vocabulary:\n"
"\n"
"- **Trigger** — something that causes or worsens a symptom\n"
"- **Relieve** — make a symptom less severe\n"
"- **Aggravate** — make a symptom worse\n"
"\n"
"## Mention Other Symptoms\n"
"\n"
"The doctor may ask whether you have noticed any other changes.\n"
"\n"
"Common questions include:\n"
"\n"
"- “Do you have a fever?”\n"
"- “Have you felt sick or vomited?”\n"
"- “Have you noticed any swelling?”\n"
"- “Have you lost your appetite?”\n"
"- “Have you experienced dizziness?”\n"
"- “Are you short of breath?”\n"
"- “Have you had difficulty sleeping?”\n"
"- “Have you noticed any changes in your weight?”\n"
"- “Do you have any weakness or numbness?”\n"
"\n"
"You can respond:\n"
"\n"
"- “I also have a mild fever.”\n"
"- “I have felt dizzy a few times.”\n"
"- “There is some swelling around the area.”\n"
"- “I have not noticed any other symptoms.”\n"
"- “My appetite is normal.”\n"
"- “I have been sleeping poorly.”\n"
"- “I feel tired, but I do not have a fever.”\n"
"\n"
"Do not leave out a symptom because you think it is unrelated. The doctor "
"can decide whether it is relevant.\n"
"\n"
"## Explain What You Have Already Tried\n"
"\n"
"Tell the doctor whether you have taken medication, changed your routine, or "
"tried another solution.\n"
"\n"
"You can say:\n"
"\n"
"- “I have been resting.”\n"
"- “I tried an over-the-counter pain reliever.”\n"
"- “I used a cold compress.”\n"
"- “I stopped exercising for several days.”\n"
"- “I changed my diet, but it did not help.”\n"
"- “The medication helped for a few hours.”\n"
"- “I have not tried anything yet.”\n"
"- “I spoke with a pharmacist before making this appointment.”\n"
"\n"
"Be ready to explain:\n"
"\n"
"- What you used\n"
"- How much you used\n"
"- How often you used it\n"
"- Whether it helped\n"
"- Whether you noticed side effects\n"
"\n"
"Do not pretend to remember details that you do not know. You can say:\n"
"\n"
"- “I don’t remember the exact name.”\n"
"- “I can show you the package.”\n"
"- “I’m not sure about the dosage.”\n"
"- “I took it according to the instructions on the label.”\n"
"\n"
"## Discuss Current Medication\n"
"\n"
"The doctor may need to know about all medication and supplements you take.\n"
"\n"
"You can say:\n"
"\n"
"- “I take this medication every morning.”\n"
"- “I use an inhaler when necessary.”\n"
"- “I recently started taking a new prescription.”\n"
"- “I stopped taking it last week.”\n"
"- “I occasionally take an over-the-counter pain reliever.”\n"
"- “I’m not currently taking any medication.”\n"
"- “I have a list of my medications with me.”\n"
"\n"
"The doctor may ask:\n"
"\n"
"- “What dose do you take?”\n"
"- “How long have you been taking it?”\n"
"- “Who prescribed it?”\n"
"- “Have you missed any doses?”\n"
"- “Have you noticed any side effects?”\n"
"\n"
"Do not stop prescribed medication based only on a language-learning "
"simulation. Discuss medication changes with a qualified healthcare "
"professional.\n"
"\n"
"## Mention Allergies\n"
"\n"
"Tell the doctor about medication, food, or other allergies.\n"
"\n"
"Useful phrases include:\n"
"\n"
"- “I’m allergic to penicillin.”\n"
"- “I have a severe peanut allergy.”\n"
"- “This medication caused a rash in the past.”\n"
"- “I do not have any known allergies.”\n"
"- “I’m not sure whether it was an allergic reaction.”\n"
"- “I experienced swelling after taking that medicine.”\n"
"\n"
"The doctor may ask what happened during the reaction and how serious it "
"was.\n"
"\n"
"## Discuss Your Medical History\n"
"\n"
"The doctor may ask about previous illnesses, injuries, operations, or "
"similar symptoms.\n"
"\n"
"You can say:\n"
"\n"
"- “I had the same problem two years ago.”\n"
"- “I have a history of back problems.”\n"
"- “I had surgery five years ago.”\n"
"- “I was treated for this previously.”\n"
"- “I have not experienced this before.”\n"
"- “I have a long-term medical condition.”\n"
"- “I was recently in hospital.”\n"
"- “I have brought my previous test results.”\n"
"\n"
"You may also be asked about health conditions in your family:\n"
"\n"
"- “Does anyone in your family have a similar condition?”\n"
"- “Is there a family history of heart disease?”\n"
"- “Do any close relatives have diabetes?”\n"
"\n"
"You can say:\n"
"\n"
"- “My father has a similar condition.”\n"
"- “There is no known family history.”\n"
"- “I’m not sure.”\n"
"- “I would need to check.”\n"
"\n"
"## Answer Lifestyle Questions\n"
"\n"
"A doctor may ask about sleep, exercise, diet, alcohol, smoking, work, or "
"stress because these factors can affect health.\n"
"\n"
"You may hear:\n"
"\n"
"- “Do you smoke?”\n"
"- “How often do you exercise?”\n"
"- “How much alcohol do you drink?”\n"
"- “How have you been sleeping?”\n"
"- “Has anything changed in your diet?”\n"
"- “Are you under unusual stress?”\n"
"- “What kind of work do you do?”\n"
"\n"
"Answer as honestly as possible. The purpose is to understand your health, "
"not to judge you.\n"
"\n"
"Useful phrases include:\n"
"\n"
"- “I do not smoke.”\n"
"- “I drink occasionally.”\n"
"- “I exercise two or three times a week.”\n"
"- “I sit at a desk for most of the day.”\n"
"- “I have been sleeping less than usual.”\n"
"- “My work has been particularly stressful recently.”\n"
"- “There have been no major lifestyle changes.”\n"
"\n"
"## Ask the Doctor to Clarify\n"
"\n"
"Medical explanations may contain unfamiliar words. It is always acceptable "
"to ask for simpler language.\n"
"\n"
"Useful phrases include:\n"
"\n"
"- “Could you explain that in simpler terms?”\n"
"- “What does that word mean?”\n"
"- “Could you repeat that more slowly?”\n"
"- “Could you write the name down?”\n"
"- “I’m not sure I understood.”\n"
"- “Does that mean the problem is serious?”\n"
"- “Could you show me where that is?”\n"
"- “Could you explain what you are checking for?”\n"
"- “Could you give me the instructions in writing?”\n"
"\n"
"To check your understanding, summarize what you heard:\n"
"\n"
"> “So you are saying that we need more information before we know what is "
"causing the problem. Is that correct?”\n"
"\n"
"## Understand Examinations and Tests\n"
"\n"
"During a consultation, a doctor may suggest an examination or test to "
"gather more information.\n"
"\n"
"Common examples include:\n"
"\n"
"- A physical examination\n"
"- A blood test\n"
"- A urine test\n"
"- A swab\n"
"- An X-ray\n"
"- An ultrasound\n"
"- Another type of scan\n"
"- A referral to a specialist\n"
"- Monitoring the symptoms for a period of time\n"
"\n"
"Useful questions include:\n"
"\n"
"- “What is this test for?”\n"
"- “How should I prepare?”\n"
"- “Will it be painful?”\n"
"- “Are there any risks?”\n"
"- “How long will it take?”\n"
"- “When will the results be available?”\n"
"- “How will I receive the results?”\n"
"- “Do I need another appointment?”\n"
"- “What happens if the result is abnormal?”\n"
"- “Is there an alternative?”\n"
"\n"
"The AI roleplay should only help you practise these conversations. It "
"should not claim that a particular test is medically necessary for your "
"real symptoms.\n"
"\n"
"## Discuss a Possible Plan\n"
"\n"
"A real doctor may explain possible next steps, but you should ask questions "
"before agreeing to anything you do not understand.\n"
"\n"
"Useful questions include:\n"
"\n"
"- “What are the next steps?”\n"
"- “What are the available options?”\n"
"- “What is the purpose of this medication?”\n"
"- “How should it be taken?”\n"
"- “Are there common side effects?”\n"
"- “Could it interact with my other medication?”\n"
"- “How soon should I expect an improvement?”\n"
"- “What should I do if it does not help?”\n"
"- “Is there anything I should avoid?”\n"
"- “Do I need to change my normal activities?”\n"
"\n"
"You can also ask:\n"
"\n"
"- “Are there any alternatives?”\n"
"- “What would happen if we waited?”\n"
"- “Do I need to decide today?”\n"
"- “Could I have some written information?”\n"
"- “Should I speak with a specialist?”\n"
"\n"
"The roleplay may practise asking these questions, but it must not prescribe "
"medication or create a real treatment plan.\n"
"\n"
"## Confirm Medication Instructions\n"
"\n"
"If a real clinician prescribes medication, confirm exactly how it should be "
"used.\n"
"\n"
"You may need to understand:\n"
"\n"
"- The medication name\n"
"- The dose\n"
"- How often to take it\n"
"- When to take it\n"
"- How long to take it\n"
"- Whether to take it with food\n"
"- Possible side effects\n"
"- What to do if you miss a dose\n"
"\n"
"Useful phrases include:\n"
"\n"
"- “How often should I take it?”\n"
"- “Should I take it before or after food?”\n"
"- “How many days should I use it?”\n"
"- “What should I do if I miss a dose?”\n"
"- “Are there any side effects I should watch for?”\n"
"- “Can I take it with my other medication?”\n"
"- “Could you write the instructions down?”\n"
"\n"
"Do not rely on the roleplay for real dosing instructions. Follow the "
"directions given by your doctor or pharmacist.\n"
"\n"
"## Ask When to Seek More Help\n"
"\n"
"Before leaving, ask what to do if the symptoms continue or become worse.\n"
"\n"
"Useful questions include:\n"
"\n"
"- “When should I contact the clinic again?”\n"
"- “What should I do if the symptoms get worse?”\n"
"- “Which changes should I watch for?”\n"
"- “When should I arrange a follow-up?”\n"
"- “Who should I contact outside normal hours?”\n"
"- “Should I go to urgent care if this happens again?”\n"
"- “How long should I wait before asking for another appointment?”\n"
"\n"
"A real healthcare professional can give safety instructions based on your "
"specific situation. A simulation cannot assess your personal medical risk.\n"
"\n"
"## Request a Referral or Follow-Up\n"
"\n"
"The doctor may recommend another appointment or referral.\n"
"\n"
"You can ask:\n"
"\n"
"- “Do I need to see a specialist?”\n"
"- “Will you send the referral?”\n"
"- “Do I need to contact the specialist myself?”\n"
"- “How long does the referral usually take?”\n"
"- “Should I book a follow-up appointment?”\n"
"- “When should I come back?”\n"
"- “Will we discuss the test results at the next visit?”\n"
"- “Can the follow-up be done by phone?”\n"
"\n"
"Before leaving, confirm who is responsible for the next action.\n"
"\n"
"## Discuss Privacy or Sensitive Topics\n"
"\n"
"Some health concerns may be difficult to discuss.\n"
"\n"
"You can say:\n"
"\n"
"- “This is difficult for me to talk about.”\n"
"- “I would prefer to discuss this privately.”\n"
"- “Could the other person leave the room?”\n"
"- “Could I speak with a doctor of a particular gender?”\n"
"- “I need a moment before I continue.”\n"
"- “I’m not sure how to describe the problem.”\n"
"- “Could I write it down instead?”\n"
"\n"
"You can also ask:\n"
"\n"
"- “Will this information remain confidential?”\n"
"- “Who will have access to my medical information?”\n"
"- “Can I bring someone I trust into the consultation?”\n"
"\n"
"## Take Notes and Check Your Understanding\n"
"\n"
"It can be difficult to remember everything discussed during an appointment.\n"
"\n"
"You can say:\n"
"\n"
"- “Do you mind if I take notes?”\n"
"- “Could you write down the important instructions?”\n"
"- “Could you repeat the name of the test?”\n"
"- “Can I receive a summary after the appointment?”\n"
"- “Let me make sure I understood correctly.”\n"
"\n"
"A useful method is to repeat the plan in your own words:\n"
"\n"
"> “So I need to complete the test, continue tracking the symptoms, and "
"arrange another appointment after the results are available. Is that "
"correct?”\n"
"\n"
"This technique helps identify misunderstandings before you leave.\n"
"\n"
"## A Simple Doctor Conversation\n"
"\n"
"A typical consultation may look like this:\n"
"\n"
"> **Doctor:** What brings you in today?  \n"
"> **Patient:** I’ve had pain in my lower back for about a week, and it is "
"not improving.  \n"
"> **Doctor:** Did it begin suddenly or gradually?  \n"
"> **Patient:** Gradually. I first noticed it after working at my desk for "
"several hours.  \n"
"> **Doctor:** How would you describe the pain?  \n"
"> **Patient:** It is usually a dull ache, but I sometimes feel a sharp pain "
"when I stand up.  \n"
"> **Doctor:** How severe is it?  \n"
"> **Patient:** About five out of ten. It is worse in the evening.  \n"
"> **Doctor:** Have you tried anything for it?  \n"
"> **Patient:** I have been resting, but that has not made much difference.  "
"\n"
"> **Doctor:** Do you have any other symptoms?  \n"
"> **Patient:** No, I have not noticed anything else.  \n"
"> **Doctor:** Thank you. I would now ask some additional questions and "
"explain what a clinician might examine.  \n"
"> **Patient:** Before we continue, could you explain what you are checking "
"for?\n"
"\n"
"## A Simple Symptom Formula\n"
"\n"
"When describing a health concern, include:\n"
"\n"
"1. **Main symptom:** What is bothering you?\n"
"2. **Location:** Where do you feel it?\n"
"3. **Onset:** When and how did it begin?\n"
"4. **Description:** What does it feel like?\n"
"5. **Severity:** How strong or disruptive is it?\n"
"6. **Pattern:** Is it constant, occasional, improving, or worsening?\n"
"7. **Triggers:** What makes it better or worse?\n"
"8. **Other symptoms:** Have you noticed anything else?\n"
"9. **Previous attempts:** What have you already tried?\n"
"10. **Questions:** What do you want the doctor to explain?\n"
"\n"
"For example:\n"
"\n"
"> “I’ve had a sharp pain in my right shoulder for ten days. It is about six "
"out of ten and becomes worse when I lift my arm. Rest helps a little, but "
"the problem is not improving.”\n"
"\n"
"## Practice Scenario\n"
"\n"
"In this roleplay, you are attending an appointment with Dr. Ash. The AI "
"doctor will help you practise describing a health concern, answering common "
"questions, and understanding the structure of a medical consultation.\n"
"\n"
"During the conversation, try to:\n"
"\n"
"- Give a short summary of your main concern.\n"
"- Explain when the symptoms started.\n"
"- Describe their location, severity, and frequency.\n"
"- Say what makes them better or worse.\n"
"- Mention any related symptoms.\n"
"- Explain what you have already tried.\n"
"- Answer general questions about medication, allergies, and medical "
"history.\n"
"- Ask for clarification when you do not understand something.\n"
"- Ask what a real patient might need to confirm about tests or next steps.\n"
"- Summarize the conversation in your own words before it ends.\n"
"\n"
"Focus on describing your experience clearly. Do not use this simulation to "
"obtain a diagnosis, medication recommendation, or treatment plan."
msgstr ""
"与医生交谈可能会很困难，特别是当你需要用另一种语言描述症状时。你可能需要解释你感觉什么，问题何时开始，有多严重，以及如何影响你的日常生活。\n"
"\n"
"你不需要高级的医学词汇。清晰的描述、具体的例子和诚实的回答通常比试图猜测病症名称更有用。\n"
"\n"
"此角色扮演仅用于语言练习。它不能诊断疾病、推荐治疗或代替专业医疗护理。如果你认为自己可能正经历医疗紧急状况，请联系当地紧急服务。\n"
"\n"
"## 开始咨询\n"
"\n"
"医生可能会开始问：\n"
"\n"
"- “你今天为什么来看病？”\n"
"- “我能帮你什么？”\n"
"- “看起来有什么问题？”\n"
"- “你能描述一下你的症状吗？”\n"
"- “什么时候开始的？”\n"
"- “你以前经历过这种情况吗？”\n"
"\n"
"你可以从简短的总结开始：\n"
"\n"
"- “我咳嗽不止大约一周了。”\n"
"- “我一直在经历下背部疼痛。”\n"
"- “最近我感到异常疲惫。”\n"
"- “我的手臂上有一片皮疹，情况没有改善。”\n"
"- “我比平时更频繁地头痛。”\n"
"- “我是来讨论我最近的检测结果的。”\n"
"- “我来跟进一个我们之前讨论的问题。”\n"
"\n"
"一个有用的开场结构是：\n"
"\n"
"> “我经历了[症状]有[时间长度]，而且它正在[改善、恶化或保持不变]。”\n"
"\n"
"例如：\n"
"\n"
"> “我经历了右肩疼痛大约十天，它似乎在恶化。”\n"
"\n"
"## 描述问题所在\n"
"\n"
"尽可能具体描述疼痛或不适的位置。\n"
"\n"
"有用的短语包括：\n"
"\n"
"- “疼痛在我的下背部。”\n"
"- “右侧胸部疼。”\n"
"- “左膝后面感到不适。”\n"
"- “疼痛从我的脖子开始，然后移动到肩膀。”\n"
"- “皮疹主要在我的手臂上。”\n"
"- “我全身感到酸痛。”\n"
"- “很难确定确切位置。”\n"
"- “疼痛似乎在移动。”\n"
"\n"
"医生可能会问：\n"
"\n"
"- “你能指出疼痛的位置吗？”\n"
"- “是一侧还是两侧？”\n"
"- “疼痛有移动到别处吗？”\n"
"- “不适是靠近表面还是更深？”\n"
"- “受影响的区域对触碰敏感吗？”\n"
"\n"
"## 解释何时开始\n"
"\n"
"医生通常会问症状何时开始，之前是否发生了事情。\n"
"\n"
"你可以说：\n"
"\n"
"- “今天早上开始的。”\n"
"- “三天前我首次注意到的。”\n"
"- “症状是逐渐开始的。”\n"
"- “运动时突然开始的。”\n"
"- “旅行回来后开始的。”\n"
"- “开始新药后我注意到的。”\n"
"- “我醒来时就有疼痛。”\n"
"- “我不记得确切何时开始的。”\n"
"\n"
"你可能听到的问题包括：\n"
"\n"
"- “是突然开始的还是逐渐的？”\n"
"- “开始时你在做什么？”\n"
"- “之前有发生什么异常情况吗？”\n"
"- “症状自那时起有改变吗？”\n"
"- “有受伤吗？”\n"
"\n"
"## 描述感觉如何\n"
"\n"
"尝试描述感觉而不仅仅说哪里疼。\n"
"\n"
"疼痛或不适的感觉可能是：\n"
"\n"
"- 锐痛\n"
"- 钝痛\n"
"- 灼烧感\n"
"- 跳痛\n"
"- 刺痛\n"
"- 酸痛\n"
"- 绞痛\n"
"- 紧绷\n"
"- 沉重\n"
"- 刺痒\n"
"- 麻木\n"
"- 发痒\n"
"- 敏感\n"
"- 触痛\n"
"\n"
"例子：\n"
"\n"
"- “感觉像是钝痛。”\n"
"- “有时候我会感到锐痛。”\n"
"- “有灼烧的感觉。”\n"
"- “我的手指感到麻木。”\n"
"- “患处发痒且敏感。”\n"
"- “感觉像压力而不是痛。”\n"
"- “我的胸部感到紧绷。”\n"
"- “我的手有刺痛感。”\n"
"\n"
"如果你不知道确切的词，用另一种方式描述：\n"
"\n"
"> “感觉像有什么东西在压迫那个区域。”\n"
"\n"
"## 解释严重程度\n"
"\n"
"医生可能会要求你在零到十的范围内对疼痛或不适进行评分。\n"
"\n"
"- **零**表示没有痛苦。\n"
"- **十**表示你能想象的最严重的痛苦。\n"
"\n"
"你可以说：\n"
"\n"
"- “疼痛大约是十级中的四级。”\n"
"- “大多数时候是轻微的。”\n"
"- “晚上会变严重。”\n"
"- “不舒服，但我还能工作。”\n"
"- “疼痛足以让我醒来。”\n"
"- “难以行走。”\n"
"- “症状影响我的日常活动。”\n"
"\n"
"还可以解释你能做或不能做的事情：\n"
"\n"
"- “我能走路，但上楼梯很痛。”\n"
"- “我无法集中注意力工作。”\n"
"- “我很难入睡。”\n"
"- “我能正常饮食。”\n"
"- “轻微活动后我就感到疲惫。”\n"
"- “我不得不停止锻炼。”\n"
"\n"
"## 解释发生的频率\n"
"\n"
"症状可能是持续的，也可能间歇出现。\n"
"\n"
"有用的短语包括：\n"
"\n"
"- “疼痛是持续的。”\n"
"- “时有时无。”\n"
"- “每天发生好几次。”\n"
"- “通常持续大约二十分钟。”\n"
"- “早上症状更严重。”\n"
"- “主要在进食后发生。”\n"
"- “只有在运动时发生。”\n"
"- “休息后我感觉好多了。”\n"
"- “似乎没有任何规律。”\n"
"\n"
"医生可能会问：\n"
"\n"
"- “每次发作持续多久？”\n"
"- “每天都发生吗？”\n"
"- “在特定时间更糟吗？”\n"
"- “有东西触发它吗？”\n"
"- “有什么能让它改善吗？”\n"
"\n"
"## 描述什么让情况好转或恶化\n"
"\n"
"解释运动、饮食、休息、药物或其他因素是否改变了症状。\n"
"\n"
"你可以说：\n"
"\n"
"- “活动时变得更糟。”\n"
"- “深呼吸时疼痛增加。”\n"
"- “休息后感觉好多了。”\n"
"- “热敷似乎有帮助。”\n"
"- \"在进餐后症状更严重。\"\n"
"- \"躺下时有所改善。\"\n"
"- \"止痛药暂时有帮助。\"\n"
"- \"似乎没有任何影响。\"\n"
"- \"感到压力时问题恶化。\"\n"
"\n"
"有用的词汇：\n"
"\n"
"- **触发**——导致或加重症状的东西\n"
"- **缓解**——使症状减轻\n"
"- **加重**——使症状更严重\n"
"\n"
"## 提及其他症状\n"
"\n"
"医生可能会询问你是否注意到了其他变化。\n"
"\n"
"常见的问题包括：\n"
"\n"
"- \"你有发烧吗？\"\n"
"- “你觉得恶心或呕吐过吗？”\n"
"- “你注意到有肿胀吗？”\n"
"- “你有没有失去食欲？”\n"
"- “你感到头晕吗？”\n"
"- “你感觉到呼吸困难吗？”\n"
"- \"你在睡眠方面有困难吗？\"\n"
"- \"你注意到体重有变化吗？\"\n"
"- \"你有任何无力或麻木的感觉吗？\"\n"
"\n"
"你可以回答：\n"
"\n"
"- \"我还有轻微发烧。\"\n"
"- \"我有几次感到头晕。\"\n"
"- \"在区域周围有一些肿胀。\"\n"
"- “我没有注意到其他症状。”\n"
"- \"我的食欲正常。\"\n"
"- \"我睡不好。\"\n"
"- “我感到疲倦，但没有发烧。”\n"
"\n"
"不要遗漏一个症状，因为你认为它与之无关。医生可以决定它是否相关。\n"
"\n"
"## 解释你已经尝试过的\n"
"\n"
"告诉医生你是否服用了药物、改变了习惯，或尝试了其他解决方案。\n"
"\n"
"你可以说：\n"
"\n"
"- “我一直在休息。”\n"
"- “我试过非处方止痛药。”\n"
"- “我用了冷敷。”\n"
"- “我停止锻炼了几天。”\n"
"- \"我改变了饮食，但没有帮助。\"\n"
"- “药物帮忙了几个小时。”\n"
"- \"我还没有尝试任何方法。\"\n"
"- \"在预约前我和药剂师进行了交谈。\"\n"
"\n"
"准备解释：\n"
"\n"
"- 你用了什么\n"
"- 用了多少\n"
"- 使用频率\n"
"- 是否有帮助\n"
"- 是否注意到副作用\n"
"\n"
"不要假装记住你不知道的细节。你可以说：\n"
"\n"
"- “我不记得确切的名称。”\n"
"- “我可以给你看包装。”\n"
"- “我不确定剂量。”\n"
"- “我根据标签上的说明服用。”\n"
"\n"
"## 讨论当前药物\n"
"\n"
"医生可能需要了解你正在服用的所有药物和补充剂。\n"
"\n"
"你可以说：\n"
"\n"
"- “我每天早上服用这种药物。”\n"
"- \"我在需要时使用吸入器。\"\n"
"- \"我最近开始服用新的处方药。\"\n"
"- \"我上周停止了服用。\"\n"
"- \"我偶尔会服用非处方止痛药。\"\n"
"- \"我目前没有服用任何药物。\"\n"
"- \"我有我的药物清单。\"\n"
"\n"
"医生可能会问：\n"
"\n"
"- \"你服用的剂量是多少？\"\n"
"- “你服用了多久？”\n"
"- \"是谁给你开的处方？\"\n"
"- “你错过过剂量吗？”\n"
"- “你注意到任何副作用吗？”\n"
"\n"
"不要仅根据语言学习模拟停止处方药物。有关药物更改，请与合格的医疗保健专业人员讨论。\n"
"\n"
"## 提及过敏\n"
"\n"
"告诉医生关于药物、食物或其他过敏的情况。\n"
"\n"
"有用的短语包括：\n"
"\n"
"- \"我对青霉素过敏。\"\n"
"- \"我有严重的花生过敏。\"\n"
"- \"这种药以前引起过皮疹。\"\n"
"- \"我没有任何已知过敏。\"\n"
"- \"我不确定这是否是过敏反应。\"\n"
"- \"服用那种药后我有肿胀迹象。\"\n"
"\n"
"医生可能会问发生反应时的情况以及有多严重。\n"
"\n"
"## 讨论你的病史\n"
"\n"
"医生可能会询问以前的疾病、受伤、手术或类似症状。\n"
"\n"
"你可以说：\n"
"\n"
"- \"两年前我有过同样的问题。\"\n"
"- \"我有背部问题的病史。\"\n"
"- \"五年前我做过手术。\"\n"
"- \"之前对此进行过治疗。\"\n"
"- \"我没有经历过这种情况。\"\n"
"- \"我有长期的医疗状况。\"\n"
"- “我最近住院过。”\n"
"- “我带来了我之前的检测结果。”\n"
"\n"
"你可能还会被询问家庭的健康状况：\n"
"\n"
"- “你的家族中有没有人有类似状况？”\n"
"- “有没有心脏病的家族史？”\n"
"- “有没有亲属有糖尿病？”\n"
"\n"
"你可以说：\n"
"\n"
"- \"我父亲有类似的状况。\"\n"
"- \"没有已知的家族史。\"\n"
"- “我不确定。”\n"
"- “我需要确认一下。”\n"
"\n"
"## 回答生活方式问题\n"
"\n"
"医生可能会询问睡眠、锻炼、饮食、酒精、吸烟、工作或压力，因为这些因素会影响健康。\n"
"\n"
"你可能会听到：\n"
"\n"
"- \"你吸烟吗？\"\n"
"- “你多长时间锻炼一次？”\n"
"- \"你喝多少酒？\"\n"
"- “你最近睡眠怎么样？”\n"
"- \"你的饮食有发生变化吗？\"\n"
"- “你有不寻常的压力吗？”\n"
"- \"你从事什么工作？\"\n"
"\n"
"尽可能诚实地回答。目的是了解你的健康状况，而不是评判你。\n"
"\n"
"有用的短语包括：\n"
"\n"
"- \"我不吸烟。\"\n"
"- \"我偶尔喝酒。\"\n"
"- \"我一周锻炼两到三次。\"\n"
"- “我大多数时候坐在桌子前。”\n"
"- \"我睡得比平常少。\"\n"
"- \"我的工作最近特别有压力。\"\n"
"- \"没有发生重大生活方式变化。\"\n"
"\n"
"## 请医生澄清\n"
"\n"
"医学解释可能包含不熟悉的词汇。要求使用更简单的语言总是可以接受的。\n"
"\n"
"有用的短语包括：\n"
"\n"
"- \"你能用简单的术语解释一下吗？\"\n"
"- \"那个词是什么意思？\"\n"
"- “你能再慢一点重复一遍吗？”\n"
"- “你能把名字写下来吗？”\n"
"- \"我不确定我是否理解了。\"\n"
"- \"那是否意味着问题很严重？\"\n"
"- \"你能告诉我那个在哪里吗？\"\n"
"- \"你能解释一下你在检查什么吗？\"\n"
"- “你能把说明写给我吗？”\n"
"\n"
"为了检查你的理解，总结一下你听到的内容：\n"
"\n"
"> “所以你是说我们需要更多信息才能知道是什么导致了这个问题。是这样吗？”\n"
"\n"
"## 了解检查和检测\n"
"\n"
"在咨询过程中，医生可能会建议进行检查或检测以收集更多信息。\n"
"\n"
"常见的例子包括：\n"
"\n"
"- 体格检查\n"
"- 血液检测\n"
"- 尿液检测\n"
"- 棉签取样\n"
"- X光\n"
"- 超声波\n"
"- 其他类型的扫描\n"
"- 转诊至专家\n"
"- 在一段时间内监测症状\n"
"\n"
"有用的问题包括：\n"
"\n"
"- \"这个检测是干什么的？\"\n"
"- \"我该如何准备？\"\n"
"- “会痛吗？”\n"
"- \"有什么风险吗？\"\n"
"- “大概要多久？”\n"
"- “什么时候能拿到结果？”\n"
"- “我会如何收到结果？”\n"
"- “我需要再预约吗？”\n"
"- \"如果结果不正常会怎样？\"\n"
"- “有没有备选方案？”\n"
"\n"
"AI角色扮演只应帮助你练习这些对话。它不应声称某个检测对你的真实症状是医学上必需的。\n"
"\n"
"## 讨论可能的计划\n"
"\n"
"真正的医生可能会解释可能的下一步，但在同意不理解的事情之前，你应该先问问题。\n"
"\n"
"有用的问题包括：\n"
"\n"
"- “下一步是什么？”\n"
"- \"有哪些可用选项？\"\n"
"- \"这种药物的目的是什么？\"\n"
"- \"应该怎么吃？\"\n"
"- \"常见的副作用是什么？\"\n"
"- \"它可能与我的其他药物相互作用吗？\"\n"
"- \"我多快应该预计看到改善？\"\n"
"- \"如果它没有帮助，我该怎么办？\"\n"
"- “我需要避免什么吗？”\n"
"- \"我需要改变我正常的活动吗？\"\n"
"\n"
"你还可以问：\n"
"\n"
"- “有没有替代方案？”\n"
"- \"如果我们等待会怎样？\"\n"
"- \"我今天需要决定吗？\"\n"
"- “我能否得到一些书面信息？”\n"
"- “我是否应该和专家交谈？”\n"
"\n"
"角色扮演可能是练习这些问题，但它不能开处方药物或创建真实的治疗方案。\n"
"\n"
"## 确认药物说明\n"
"\n"
"如果真正的医生开了药，确认它的确使用方法。\n"
"\n"
"你可能需要理解：\n"
"\n"
"- 药物的名称\n"
"- 剂量\n"
"- 服用频率\n"
"- 何时服用\n"
"- 服用多长时间\n"
"- 是否需要随餐服用\n"
"- 可能的副作用\n"
"- 如果错过剂量该怎么办\n"
"\n"
"有用的短语包括：\n"
"\n"
"- \"我多久应该服用一次？\"\n"
"- \"应该饭前还是饭后服用？\"\n"
"- \"我应该服用几天？\"\n"
"- \"如果我错过了一次剂量，我该怎么办？\"\n"
"- \"我应该注意哪些副作用？\"\n"
"- \"我能与我的其他药物一起服用吗？\"\n"
"- “你能把说明写下来吗？”\n"
"\n"
"不要依赖角色扮演提供真实的剂量说明。请遵循医生或药剂师给出的指示。\n"
"\n"
"## 询问何时寻求更多帮助\n"
"\n"
"在离开之前，询问如果症状继续或变得更严重该怎么做。\n"
"\n"
"有用的问题包括：\n"
"\n"
"- “我什么时候应该再次联系诊所？”\n"
"- \"如果症状变严重，我该怎么办？\"\n"
"- \"我应该注意哪些变化？\"\n"
"- \"我应该何时安排后续？\"\n"
"- “在正常工作时间以外，我该联系谁？”\n"
"- \"如果这种情况再次发生，我应该去急诊吗？\"\n"
"- \"我应该等多长时间再安排预约？\"\n"
"\n"
"真正的医疗保健专业人员可以根据你的具体情况提供安全指示。模拟不能评估你个人的医疗风险。\n"
"\n"
"## 请求转诊或跟进\n"
"\n"
"医生可能会建议另一个预约或转诊。\n"
"\n"
"你可以问：\n"
"\n"
"- “我需要见专家吗？”\n"
"- \"你会发送转诊吗？\"\n"
"- “我需要自己联系专家吗？”\n"
"- \"转诊通常需要多长时间？\"\n"
"- \"我应该预约后续吗？\"\n"
"- \"我应该何时回来？\"\n"
"- \"我们会在下次拜访时讨论测试结果吗？\"\n"
"- “跟进可以通过电话进行吗？”\n"
"\n"
"在离开之前，确认谁负责进行下一个行动。\n"
"\n"
"## 讨论隐私或敏感话题\n"
"\n"
"某些健康问题可能难以讨论。\n"
"\n"
"你可以说：\n"
"\n"
"- \"这对我来说很难说。\"\n"
"- \"我更愿意私下讨论。\"\n"
"- \"那个其他人可以离开房间吗？\"\n"
"- \"我可以和某个性别的医生交谈吗？\"\n"
"- “在继续之前我需要片刻时间。”\n"
"- \"我不确定如何描述问题。\"\n"
"- \"我可以写下来吗？\"\n"
"\n"
"你还可以问：\n"
"\n"
"- \"这些信息会保密吗？\"\n"
"- “谁能访问我的医疗信息？”\n"
"- \"我可以带我信任的人进入咨询室吗？\"\n"
"\n"
"## 记录和检查你的理解\n"
"\n"
"很难记住预约过程中讨论的所有内容。\n"
"\n"
"你可以说：\n"
"\n"
"- \"你介意我做笔记吗？\"\n"
"- \"你能否写下重要的指示？\"\n"
"- \"你能否重复一下测试的名称？\"\n"
"- \"预约后我可以收到摘要吗？\"\n"
"- \"让我确保我理解正确：\"\n"
"\n"
"一个有用的方法是用自己的话重复计划：\n"
"\n"
"> “所以我需要完成测试、继续跟踪症状，并在结果出来后再安排一个预约。是这样吗？”\n"
"\n"
"这个技巧有助于在离开前确定误解。\n"
"\n"
"## 简单的医生对话\n"
"\n"
"一个典型的咨询可能是这样的：\n"
"\n"
"> **医生:** 你今天为什么来看病？  \n"
"> **病人:** 我的下背部疼痛已经有大约一周了，而且没有改善。  \n"
"> **医生:** 是突然开始的还是逐渐的？  \n"
"> **病人:** 逐渐的。我首先是注意到在工作了几小时后。  \n"
"> **医生:** 你会如何描述疼痛？  \n"
"> **病人:** 通常是钝痛，但有时站起来时会感到锐痛。  \n"
"> **医生:** 有多严重？  \n"
"> **病人:** 十级中的五级。晚上更严重。  \n"
"> **医生:** 你试过什么方法吗？  \n"
"> **病人:** 我一直在休息，但没有太大改观。  \n"
"> **医生:** 你还有其他症状吗？  \n"
"> **病人:** 不，我没有注意到其他的东西。  \n"
"> **医生:** 谢谢。我现在会问一些补充问题，并解释临床医生可能会检查的内容。  \n"
"> **病人:** 在我们继续之前，你能解释一下你在检查什么吗？\n"
"\n"
"## 简单的症状公式\n"
"\n"
"描述健康问题时，包括：\n"
"\n"
"1. **主要症状：** 什么困扰着你？\n"
"2. **位置：** 你感觉在哪里？\n"
"3. **起始：** 何时和如何开始？\n"
"4. **描述：** 感觉像什么？\n"
"5. **严重程度：** 它有多强或多干扰？\n"
"6. **模式：** 是否持续、偶发、改善或恶化？\n"
"7. **触发：** 什么让它好转或恶化？\n"
"8. **其他症状：** 你注意到其他东西了吗？\n"
"9. **先前尝试：** 你已经尝试过什么？\n"
"10. **问题：** 你想让医生解释什么？\n"
"\n"
"例如：\n"
"\n"
"> “我右肩有尖锐的疼痛十天了。大约是十级中的六级，当我抬手时更糟。休息有点帮助，但问题没有改善。”\n"
"\n"
"## 练习场景\n"
"\n"
"在这个角色扮演中，你在预约与Dr. Ash的面谈。AI医生将帮助你练习描述健康问题、回答常见问题，以及了解医疗咨询的结构。\n"
"\n"
"在谈话过程中，尝试：\n"
"\n"
"- 简要总结你的主要关注点。\n"
"- 解释症状何时开始。\n"
"- 描述其位置、严重程度和频率。\n"
"- 说出让事情好转或恶化的因素。\n"
"- 提及任何相关症状。\n"
"- 解释你已经尝试过的。\n"
"- 回答关于药物、过敏和医疗史的一般问题。\n"
"- 在不理解时请求澄清。\n"
"- 问一个真正的病人可能需要确认的关于检查或后续步骤的问题。\n"
"- 在谈话结束前用自己的话总结谈话。\n"
"\n"
"专注于清晰地描述你的经历。不要利用此模拟来获得诊断，药物建议或治疗计划。"

#: src/features/RolePlay/scenarios/small-talk-with-a-stranger.ts:17
#. js-lingui-explicit-id
msgid ""
"Talking to a stranger can feel uncomfortable because neither person knows "
"what to say yet. The goal is not to sound especially clever or interesting. "
"Your first goal is simply to make the other person feel comfortable and "
"discover something you can talk about together.\n"
"\n"
"The advice below is inspired by ideas from Charles Duhigg’s book "
"*Supercommunicators*.\n"
"\n"
"## How to Start a Conversation\n"
"\n"
"Use the situation you already share. You do not need an original or "
"impressive opening line.\n"
"\n"
"Try making a simple observation and adding an easy question:\n"
"\n"
"- “This place is busier than I expected. Have you been here before?”\n"
"- “How do you know the host?”\n"
"- “What brought you to this event?”\n"
"- “That looks interesting. What are you drinking?”\n"
"- “Is this your first time at an event like this?”\n"
"\n"
"Questions about the immediate situation feel natural because they give the "
"other person an obvious way to respond.\n"
"\n"
"## Ask Questions That Reveal More Than Facts\n"
"\n"
"Basic questions are useful for starting a conversation:\n"
"\n"
"- “Where are you from?”\n"
"- “What do you do?”\n"
"- “Do you live nearby?”\n"
"\n"
"However, a conversation becomes more interesting when you gently ask about "
"the person’s experiences, feelings, or preferences.\n"
"\n"
"Instead of only asking:\n"
"\n"
"> “What do you do for work?”\n"
"\n"
"You could continue with:\n"
"\n"
"> “What do you enjoy most about it?”\n"
"\n"
"Instead of:\n"
"\n"
"> “How long have you lived here?”\n"
"\n"
"Try:\n"
"\n"
"> “What do you like most about living here?”\n"
"\n"
"Other useful questions include:\n"
"\n"
"- “How did you become interested in that?”\n"
"- “What has been the best part of your week?”\n"
"- “What do you usually enjoy doing on weekends?”\n"
"- “What is something you are looking forward to?”\n"
"- “What kind of places do you like visiting?”\n"
"\n"
"These questions are personal enough to create a connection, but still safe "
"and appropriate for someone you have just met.\n"
"\n"
"## Listen for the Kind of Conversation They Want\n"
"\n"
"People do not always want the same thing from a conversation. They may want "
"to:\n"
"\n"
"1. Exchange information or make a decision.\n"
"2. Share an emotion or experience.\n"
"3. Talk about their interests, identity, or relationships.\n"
"\n"
"Try to respond to the kind of conversation the other person has started.\n"
"\n"
"If someone says:\n"
"\n"
"> “I moved here two weeks ago and everything still feels unfamiliar.”\n"
"\n"
"A purely practical response might be:\n"
"\n"
"> “You should download a city guide.”\n"
"\n"
"But an emotional response may connect better:\n"
"\n"
"> “That sounds exciting, but probably a little overwhelming too. How has it "
"been so far?”\n"
"\n"
"You do not always need to solve a problem. Sometimes people simply want to "
"feel understood.\n"
"\n"
"## Show That You Are Really Listening\n"
"\n"
"A useful technique is to:\n"
"\n"
"1. Ask a question.\n"
"2. Listen carefully.\n"
"3. Summarize the important part in your own words.\n"
"4. Check whether you understood correctly.\n"
"\n"
"For example:\n"
"\n"
"> “So you started learning photography because you wanted a creative hobby "
"outside work. Did I understand that correctly?”\n"
"\n"
"You do not need to do this formally after every answer. Short natural "
"responses also work:\n"
"\n"
"- “So that is what brought you here.”\n"
"- “It sounds like you really enjoyed it.”\n"
"- “I can see why that was frustrating.”\n"
"- “So you prefer quieter places?”\n"
"- “That makes sense.”\n"
"\n"
"These responses show that you are not simply waiting for your turn to "
"speak.\n"
"\n"
"## Share Something About Yourself Too\n"
"\n"
"Good small talk should not feel like an interview. After asking a question, "
"share a related detail about yourself.\n"
"\n"
"For example:\n"
"\n"
"> “What kind of music do you listen to? I’ve been listening to a lot of "
"jazz recently, but I’m trying to discover something new.”\n"
"\n"
"Or:\n"
"\n"
"> “Have you travelled anywhere interesting recently? I visited a small town "
"by a lake last weekend and really enjoyed it.”\n"
"\n"
"A useful pattern is:\n"
"\n"
"**Ask → Listen → Respond → Share → Ask again**\n"
"\n"
"This creates a balanced conversation in which both people gradually reveal "
"more about themselves.\n"
"\n"
"## How to Keep the Conversation Going\n"
"\n"
"Listen for a detail you can explore.\n"
"\n"
"If the person says:\n"
"\n"
"> “I recently started running.”\n"
"\n"
"You could ask:\n"
"\n"
"- “What made you start?”\n"
"- “Do you prefer running alone or with other people?”\n"
"- “Was it difficult in the beginning?”\n"
"- “Are you training for anything?”\n"
"\n"
"You do not need to introduce a completely new topic after every answer. "
"Often, the easiest way to continue is to become curious about one detail "
"the person has already mentioned.\n"
"\n"
"## What to Avoid\n"
"\n"
"Try not to:\n"
"\n"
"- Ask many unrelated questions one after another.\n"
"- Turn every answer back to yourself.\n"
"- Give advice before understanding what the person needs.\n"
"- Ask questions that are too personal too quickly.\n"
"- Pretend to understand when you are confused.\n"
"- Focus so much on perfect grammar that you stop listening.\n"
"\n"
"It is completely natural to pause, search for a word, or ask someone to "
"repeat themselves.\n"
"\n"
"Useful phrases include:\n"
"\n"
"- “Sorry, could you say that again?”\n"
"- “What do you mean by that?”\n"
"- “I’m not sure I understood correctly.”\n"
"- “How would you describe it?”\n"
"- “Give me a second—I’m trying to find the right word.”\n"
"\n"
"## How to End the Conversation Politely\n"
"\n"
"Not every conversation needs to last a long time. You can finish warmly "
"without making the moment awkward:\n"
"\n"
"- “It was really nice talking to you.”\n"
"- “I’m going to get another drink, but I’m glad we met.”\n"
"- “I’ll let you talk to the others, but I enjoyed our conversation.”\n"
"- “I hope you enjoy the rest of the event.”\n"
"- “Maybe I’ll see you again later.”\n"
"\n"
"Ending a conversation politely is also an important communication skill.\n"
"\n"
"## A Simple Conversation Formula\n"
"\n"
"When you are unsure what to do, remember:\n"
"\n"
"1. **Open:** Comment on the shared situation.\n"
"2. **Explore:** Ask about an experience, preference, or feeling.\n"
"3. **Listen:** Respond to something specific they said.\n"
"4. **Connect:** Share a related detail about yourself.\n"
"5. **Continue or close:** Ask a follow-up question or end politely.\n"
"\n"
"## Practice Scenario\n"
"\n"
"In this roleplay, you are meeting a friendly stranger at a social event. "
"Start with an easy question, listen for interesting details, and gradually "
"move from basic facts to experiences and interests.\n"
"\n"
"Try to practice at least three skills:\n"
"\n"
"- Ask one question about an experience or feeling.\n"
"- Refer to something the stranger said earlier.\n"
"- Share something related about yourself.\n"
"\n"
"Do not worry about creating a perfect conversation. Concentrate on being "
"curious, attentive, and easy to talk to."
msgstr ""
"与陌生人交谈可能会让人感到不舒服，因为双方尚不知道该说什么。目标不是让自己听起来特别聪明或有趣。您的第一个目标仅仅是让对方感到舒适，并发现一些可以一起讨"
"论的话题。\n"
"\n"
"下面的建议灵感来源于查尔斯·杜希格的书 *超級交流者* 中的想法。\n"
"\n"
"## 如何开始对话\n"
"\n"
"利用您已经分享的情况。您不需要一个新颖或令人印象深刻的开场白。\n"
"\n"
"尝试做出一个简单的观察，并加入一个轻松的问题：\n"
"\n"
"- “这个地方比我想象的更忙。你以前来过这里吗？”\n"
"- “你是怎么认识主人的？”\n"
"- “是什么让你来到这个活动的？”\n"
"- “这看起来很有趣。你在喝什么？”\n"
"- “这是你第一次参加这样的活动吗？”\n"
"\n"
"关于眼前情况的问题感觉自然，因为它们为对方提供了显而易见的回应方式。\n"
"\n"
"## 提问超越简单事实\n"
"\n"
"基本问题对于开始对话是有用的：\n"
"\n"
"- “你来自哪里？”\n"
"- “你是做什么的？”\n"
"- “你住得近吗？”\n"
"\n"
"然而，当您温和地询问对方的经历、感受或者偏好时，对话会变得更加有趣。\n"
"\n"
"不只是问：\n"
"\n"
"> “你的工作是做什么的？”\n"
"\n"
"您可以继续问：\n"
"\n"
"> “你最喜欢这份工作的哪一点？”\n"
"\n"
"代替问：\n"
"\n"
"> “你在这里住了多久？”\n"
"\n"
"尝试：\n"
"\n"
"> “你最喜欢住在这里的什么？”\n"
"\n"
"其他有用的问题包括：\n"
"\n"
"- “你是怎么对这个感兴趣的？”\n"
"- “你这一周中最棒的部分是什么？”\n"
"- “你通常在周末喜欢做什么？”\n"
"- “有什么是你期待的吗？”\n"
"- “你喜欢去什么样的地方？”\n"
"\n"
"这些问题足够个人化以建立联系，但仍然对一个刚认识的人来说是安全和适宜的。\n"
"\n"
"## 聆听他们希望进行的对话类型\n"
"\n"
"人们并不总是希望从对话中得到同样的东西。他们可能想要：\n"
"\n"
"1. 交换信息或做出决定。\n"
"2. 分享情感或经历。\n"
"3. 讨论他们的兴趣、身份或关系。\n"
"\n"
"尝试回应对方已经开始的对话类型。\n"
"\n"
"如果有人说：\n"
"\n"
"> “我两周前搬到这里，一切仍然感到陌生。”\n"
"\n"
"一个纯粹务实的回应可能是：\n"
"\n"
"> “你应该下载一个城市指南。”\n"
"\n"
"但一个情感上的回应可能更能连接：\n"
"\n"
"> “那听起来很令人兴奋，但可能也有点压抑。到目前为止感觉如何？”\n"
"\n"
"您并不总是需要解决问题。有时候人们只是想感到被理解。\n"
"\n"
"## 表现出你确实在倾听\n"
"\n"
"一个有用的技巧是：\n"
"\n"
"1. 提问。\n"
"2. 仔细倾听。\n"
"3. 用自己的话总结重要部分。\n"
"4. 检查您是否理解正确。\n"
"\n"
"例如：\n"
"\n"
"> “所以你开始学习摄影是因为你想在工作之外寻找一种创造性的爱好。我的理解对吗？”\n"
"\n"
"您不必在每一个答案后正式地这样做。简短自然的回应同样有效：\n"
"\n"
"- “这就是让你来这里的原因。”\n"
"- “听起来你真的很享受它。”\n"
"- “我能理解为什么这会让人沮丧。”\n"
"- “所以你喜欢安静的地方？”\n"
"- “那说得通。”\n"
"\n"
"这些回应表明您不是简单地在等待轮到您说话。\n"
"\n"
"## 也分享一些关于自己的信息\n"
"\n"
"良好的闲聊不应该让人感觉像在接受采访。提问后，分享一个关于自己的相关细节。\n"
"\n"
"例如：\n"
"\n"
"> “你喜欢听什么类型的音乐？我最近听了很多爵士乐，但我正在尝试发现一些新的。”\n"
"\n"
"或者：\n"
"\n"
"> “你最近去过什么有趣的地方吗？我上周去了一座湖边的小镇，真的很喜欢那里。”\n"
"\n"
"一个有用的模式是：\n"
"\n"
"**提问 → 倾听 → 回应 → 分享 → 再次提问**\n"
"\n"
"这为一个平衡的对话创造了条件，让双方逐渐更多地透露自己的信息。\n"
"\n"
"## 如何保持对话的进行\n"
"\n"
"请注意您可以探讨的细节。\n"
"\n"
"如果对方说：\n"
"\n"
"> “我最近开始跑步。”\n"
"\n"
"您可以问：\n"
"\n"
"- “是什么让你开始的？”\n"
"- “你喜欢一个人跑步还是和别人一起？”\n"
"- “一开始困难吗？”\n"
"- “你在为什么做训练？”\n"
"\n"
"您不需要在每个答案后引入一个全新的话题。通常，继续的方法是对对方已经提到的一个细节保持好奇。\n"
"\n"
"## 应避免的事情\n"
"\n"
"尽量不要：\n"
"\n"
"- 一个接一个地问许多不相关的问题。\n"
"- 把每个答案都转回自己。\n"
"- 在了解对方需求之前给出建议。\n"
"- 太快地问过于私人化的问题。\n"
"- 在困惑时假装理解。\n"
"- 如此专注于完美的语法以至于停止倾听。\n"
"\n"
"暂停、寻找一个词或请求对方重复是完全自然的。\n"
"\n"
"有用的短语包括：\n"
"\n"
"- “抱歉，你能再说一遍吗？”\n"
"- “你这是什么意思？”\n"
"- “我不确定我是否理解正确。”\n"
"- “你会怎么描述它？”\n"
"- “给我几秒钟——我在找合适的词。”\n"
"\n"
"## 如何礼貌地结束对话\n"
"\n"
"并非每次对话都需要持续很长时间。您可以在不让场面尴尬的情况下温暖地结束：\n"
"\n"
"- “很高兴和你聊天。”\n"
"- “我要再去拿一杯饮料，但很高兴认识你。”\n"
"- “我让你和其他人聊天，但我享受我们的对话。”\n"
"- “希望你享受接下来的活动。”\n"
"- “也许稍后我会再见到你。”\n"
"\n"
"礼貌地结束对话也是一种重要的沟通技巧。\n"
"\n"
"## 一个简单的对话公式\n"
"\n"
"当您不确定要做什么时，请记住：\n"
"\n"
"1. **开头：** 评论共同的情况。\n"
"2. **探索：** 询问某个经历、偏好或感受。\n"
"3. **倾听：** 对他们所说的具体内容作出回应。\n"
"4. **连接：** 分享一个关于自己的相关细节。\n"
"5. **继续或结束：** 提出后续问题或礼貌地结束。\n"
"\n"
"## 练习场景\n"
"\n"
"在这个角色扮演中，您在社交活动中遇到了一位友好的陌生人。以一个简单的问题开始，倾听有趣的细节，并逐渐从基本事实过渡到经验和兴趣。\n"
"\n"
"尝试至少练习三个技能：\n"
"\n"
"- 询问一个关于经历或感受的问题。\n"
"- 提到陌生人早些时候说的一些事。\n"
"- 分享一些与自己相关的信息。\n"
"\n"
"不用担心创造一个完美的对话。专注于保持好奇、周到且易于交谈。"

